                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the signals`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Three events landed in one week: a leaked synergy slide citing <strong>~6% headcount</strong>, an org chart with <strong>90 duplicated roles</strong>, and a revealed <strong>14% pay-band gap</strong>. The pulse fell from 72 to 51 and negative-emotion comments jumped from 20% to 58%. Read this through an emotional-events lens: behaviour follows the <em>emotions events provoke</em>, not the merger’s merits. Separate the acute affective shock (the slide) from the chronic strain (pay inequity), find which emotion truly leads the 19% senior-engineer attrition, and treat the angry comments as data — not noise to mute.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Restore fairness`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `You have a single ring-fenced fund of <strong>£1.2M</strong> inside the freeze. Allocate it across the three justice types: <strong>distributive</strong> (harmonise pay, ~£850K, the loudest grievance), <strong>procedural</strong> (transparent criteria + appeals, ~£120K) and <strong>interactional</strong> (a manager explain-and-listen cascade, ~£200K). The trap: the unfairness is loudest on pay, but exit interviews say people leave over <em>“being told nothing and trusting no one”</em> — procedural and interactional justice move trust faster and cheaper. Decide what the 210 senior engineers feel first, and defend what you did NOT fund.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Enable job crafting under the freeze`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `You cannot promote or pay your way out. The near-zero-cost lever is <strong>job crafting</strong> — letting people reshape the task, relational and cognitive boundaries of their roles to restore meaning. But unmanaged crafting lets the unloved-but-critical <em>compliance and QA work</em> go uncovered, while crafting that needs committee sign-off for everything isn’t crafting at all. Choose who is offered a crafting conversation versus a defined role, set the guardrails that keep critical tasks owned, decide what managers can approve alone, and keep the spend under <strong>£150K</strong>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Stabilise & report to the board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Integrate Rounds 1–3 into a board-ready plan tied to the four KPIs. The board cares about the <strong>£9M synergy</strong>; show that fairness and crafting <em>protect</em> it by preventing the attrition and project stalls that would destroy it — 19% on 210 engineers at £95K each is real cash. This morning the leaked synergy slide reaches a national engineering-trade publication. Respond without contradicting the Day-1 “no compulsory redundancies” promise, and commit to the metric: <strong>sub-10% attrition by 15 May</strong>. Accept forced redundancies now and you manufacture the exact betrayal that caused the fear that caused the attrition.`,

                    r1_trigger_label:       `Which emotional event is the leading indicator of the 19% senior-engineer attrition?`,
                    r1_separate_label:      `How do you separate acute affective events from chronic structural strain?`,
                    r1_segment_label:       `Which segment’s emotional event do you respond to first?`,
