                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_comms:         `<strong>That’s the leading indicator.</strong> The exit interviews don’t say “I left for 14% more pay” — they say “I was told nothing and trusted no one.” It’s the breach of the 30-year no-layoffs promise that turns fear into exit. Read the betrayal, not just the loudest complaint.`,
                    coach_r1_pay:           `<strong>The loudest grievance is rarely the highest-leverage one.</strong> The 14% gap is real, but people aren’t resigning over it — they’re resigning over silence and broken trust. Treat pay as one signal among several, not the diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r1_org:           `<strong>The duplicated roles are a real source of chronic anxiety</strong> — but the engineers who are actually leaving cite broken trust and silence. The org chart is the structure; the betrayal of the promise is the emotion driving exit.`,
                    coach_r1_slide:         `<strong>The leaked slide is an acute affective shock, not the chronic driver of exit.</strong> It spiked fear in one week, but people leave over the sustained sense of betrayal and silence. Don’t mistake the spike for the trend.`,
                    coach_r1_acute:         `<strong>Chasing only the acute spike misses the chronic strain.</strong> The leaked slide grabbed the headline, but the pay inequity and the broken promise are the slow burn that keeps people walking. Read both, separately.`,
                    coach_r1_lump:          `<strong>One falling “sentiment” number hides the diagnosis.</strong> Tarn fairness is 29% and Halden’s is 41%; the anger is concentrated in the 90 duplicated roles. A single average tells you nothing about which segment’s emotion to answer.`,
                    coach_r1_halden:        `<strong>You answered the larger, calmer group first.</strong> Halden staff are the legacy culture, but the attrition risk and the 29% fairness score sit on the Tarn side and in the duplicated roles. You’re over-serving the calm and under-serving the angry.`,
                    coach_r1_allsegment:    `<strong>One all-company message under-serves the angriest and over-serves the calm.</strong> The Tarn-side and duplicated-role staff carry the attrition risk; a one-size reassurance won’t reach the emotion that’s driving exit. Which segment are you actually responding to?`,
                    coach_r1_suppress:      `<strong>You launched an upbeat campaign and stopped publishing the pulse free-text.</strong> The 58% negative comments didn’t disappear — they moved to a private WhatsApp group and to recruiters’ inboxes. In an emotional-events view, silencing the signal cost you the data that guides intervention.`,
                    coach_r2_payspree:      `<strong>You spent most of the fund harmonising pay.</strong> The pulse barely moved — people are leaving over “being told nothing and trusting no one,” which is procedural and interactional, not distributive. The most expensive justice type bought you the least trust. What did you have left to rebuild it?`,
                    coach_r2_processfirst:  `<strong>Process and voice first is the evidence-based play.</strong> Transparent criteria, an appeals route and a manager explain-and-listen cascade move trust faster and cheaper than levelling pay. You’re buying the justice that actually moves the attrition.`,
                    coach_r2_mixed:         `<strong>A spread across the three justice types is defensible</strong> — but tilt it. The more weight on procedural and interactional justice and the less on expensive pay harmonisation, the more trust each pound buys.`,
                    coach_r2_money:         `<strong>Money first assumes the grievance is distributive.</strong> A bigger payslip doesn’t answer “I was told nothing.” The senior engineers need voice and a credible process before they need a pay rise — that’s what moves the exit decision.`,
                    coach_r2_silence:       `<strong>Leaving the 210 engineers with nothing specific is the most expensive choice of all.</strong> They are the billable core; each departure is ~£95K plus a stalled project. Silence to the segment carrying the attrition risk is how the 19% becomes 25%.`,
                    coach_r3_all:           `<strong>Letting all 90 freely redesign their own roles feels generous</strong> — but now nobody owns the unglamorous compliance and QA tasks that keep projects billable. Free-for-all crafting drops critical work. Where is the guardrail?`,
                    coach_r3_none:          `<strong>Assigning everyone a defined role from the top isn’t crafting at all.</strong> Under a freeze, crafting is your near-zero-cost lever for meaning — switch it off and engagement stays flat while the duplicated-role anxiety festers.`,
                    coach_r3_unbounded:     `<strong>No guardrails means the unloved-but-critical work goes uncovered.</strong> Version A of the trap: everyone redesigns freely and the compliance and QA tasks that keep projects billable quietly fall off everyone’s plate. Free the meaning, but own the critical tasks first.`,
                    coach_r3_strangled:     `<strong>If every crafting request needs committee sign-off, it isn’t crafting.</strong> Version B of the trap: control so tight that engagement doesn’t move. Crafting only works when people have real latitude inside clear bounds.`,
                    coach_r3_overfree:      `<strong>Managers approving everything with no escalation is unbounded crafting by another name.</strong> Role-grade and pay changes still need a check; task and relational crafting are where you delegate. Otherwise critical work and pay consistency both drift.`,
                    coach_r3_nodeleg:       `<strong>Escalating every change strangles the lever.</strong> If managers can’t approve even task and relational crafting, nothing moves fast enough to restore meaning before people decide to leave. Delegate within the guardrails.`,
                    coach_r3_overspend:     `<strong>Job crafting is meant to be near-zero-cost.</strong> Spending well above £150K signals you’re paying your way out rather than enabling meaning — and under a freeze the integration committee will ask why. Keep it light and structural.`,
                    coach_r3_ok:            `<strong>Bounded, delegated, low-cost crafting is the lever.</strong> A targeted offer with critical tasks owned first, managers empowered within clear bounds, and a modest spend — that restores meaning without dropping the work that keeps projects billable.`,
                    coach_r4_cash:          `<strong>That’s the board’s language.</strong> 19% attrition on 210 engineers at £95K each plus stalled projects is real cash — staying below 10% protects billings and the £9M synergy. Fairness and crafting don’t spend against the synergy; they buy it.`,
                    coach_r4_soft:          `<strong>“The humane thing to do” isn’t a board case.</strong> They asked how it protects the £9M synergy and you offered a virtue, not a number. Reframe: how does fairness BUY the synergy rather than spend against it?`,
                    coach_r4_comply:        `<strong>You gave the board the headcount cut.</strong> Cutting the 90 duplicated roles hits the synergy number and breaks the Day-1 no-layoffs promise outright — manufacturing the exact betrayal that caused the fear that caused the attrition. The 1,200 who stay are watching.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>Denying the figure and re-running the upbeat campaign is signal suppression at the worst moment.</strong> The slide is already public; a contradicted denial confirms the betrayal and contradicts Day-1. The comments move to recruiters’ inboxes, not away.`,
                    coach_r4_silence:       `<strong>No comment hands the trade publication the narrative.</strong> Silence the morning the slide breaks reads as confirmation. Reaffirm the promise and show the plan, or the story runs without your side of it five weeks before the board review.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>“Improving sentiment” isn’t a commitment the board can hold you to.</strong> Without a hard sub-10% attrition number tied to the KPIs, the board defaults to the redundancies it already wanted. Commit to the metric.`,
                    coach_r4_accept:        `<strong>You concluded you can’t hit sub-10%, so you’re recommending the redundancies the board wanted anyway.</strong> You’ve manufactured the exact betrayal that caused the fear that caused the attrition. Breaking the Day-1 promise tells the 1,200 who stay that the promise was never real.`,

